p=root/'thm-the-degenerate-cyclotomic-hecke-g-zero-is-the-dual-highest-weight-module.md';s=p.read_text()
s=s.replace('[[def-lower-global-basis-of-a-quantum-highest-weight-module]];\n', '[[def-lower-global-basis-of-a-quantum-highest-weight-module]];\n')
mark='## Facts & Assumptions'
cutstatement=r'''For an arbitrary admitted consecutive $I$, the same statements hold blockwise and sum to the same lattice identifications. Use finite letter cuts containing every charge and finite root cuts containing the selected content and every intermediate content in the chosen residue words. The transition maps append the common ground tail below the lower cut and leave all letters above the upper cut absent. They fix the intrinsic Hecke generators, spectral idempotents and residue bimodules on the selected contents, and identify both integral lattices by their labelled bases. The dual module here is the restricted, weightwise dual, not the unrestricted algebraic dual of an infinite-dimensional module.

'''
s=s.replace(mark,cutstatement+mark,1)
s=s.replace('1.3 **The O-side Grothendieck group is the integral Fock module.**', '1.3 **The O-side Grothendieck group is the integral Fock module.**')
s=s.replace('while the costandard lattice is generated by the ground vector $D_{A_\\Lambda}$ over $\\mathbb Q$ and is identified with the form-dual family by the same theorem.', 'while the costandard lattice is its full weightwise $C_0$-dual: at $s=1$, $C_0(P_A,D_B)=\\delta_{AB}$ identifies it with $\\bigoplus_\\alpha\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb Z}(V(\\Lambda)_{\\mathbb Z,\\alpha},\\mathbb Z)$. In particular it is stable under divided powers, since the $e_i^{(n)}$ and $f_i^{(n)}$ are adjoint and preserve the lower lattice. Nondegeneracy identifies their rational scalar extensions, but does not identify the two integral lattices.')
s=s.replace('7.1 **Conclusion.**',r'''6.2 **The maps for arbitrary consecutive $I$.** Fix a finite set of contents and residue words. Choose $J=[k,r]$ so that $[k,r+1]$ contains every charge and $J$ contains every intermediate root support; decrease $k$ further to reach the needed stable range. The Hecke-side map is literally the identity on $H_d(m)e_\alpha$: its spectral idempotent is the same sum over words of content $\alpha$, independently of whether the ambient supported interval is $I$ or $J$. It therefore fixes the intrinsic residue corner $u_{\alpha,i}$ and the bimodules defining $e_i,f_i$. On tableaux the lower transition adjoins the prescribed ground entries to every column, and the upper transition regards the same entries in a larger alphabet with no new top entries. Removing that common tail is the inverse on the selected weight. Column ground differences, hence intrinsic Specht partitions, are unchanged. The actual finite-cut compatibility of [[thm-standard-tableau-monomials-form-a-basis-of-the-highest-weight-fock-submodule]] and [[thm-the-highest-weight-fock-submodule-has-the-qg-global-bases]] gives $D_{\jmath A}=\jmath D_A$, $S_{\jmath B}=\jmath S_B$, $P_{\jmath A}=\jmath P_A$ and commuting normalized highest projections. Thus both upper and lower lattices are identified by their full bases, not just their rational spans. On overlapping cuts these maps compose by adjoining/removing the same ground entries and by the intrinsic Hecke identity; the normalized projections agree by highest-vector uniqueness. Every content, vector and finite residue word fits such a cut. Applying steps 1.1–6.1 there and taking the compatible weightwise direct sum proves all clauses for arbitrary admitted $I$. No full degree algebra is identified with a proper supported cut. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.3, step 3.1, given, construct, algebra]

7.1 **Conclusion.**''')
s=s.replace('as asserted in (v). No step', 'as asserted in (v); step 6.2 supplies the arbitrary-$I$ maps and full integral extension. No step')
